```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
The fault is entirely on dns_server: the BIND9 named daemon (the authoritative
DNS service) is not running. When host_1_1_1_1 tries to resolve "web0.local"
or "web1.local", its resolver contacts 10.200.0.2:53 per /etc/resolv.conf, but
receives "connection refused" because no process is listening on that port. The
OSPF routing fabric is fully healthy — a direct ping from host_1_1_1_1 to
10.200.0.2 shows 0% loss and all core routers carry a valid OSPF route to
10.200.0.0/24 — so the team's hypothesis about a broken core path is a
false premise.

The named.service unit is marked enabled but reported inactive (dead), and
journalctl shows no entries, indicating the daemon exited silently at some point
after lab startup (systemd in this container environment starts it but fails to
keep it alive). The configuration and zone data are sound: named-checkconf
returns exit 0, and db.local correctly maps web0 → 10.200.0.3 and
web1 → 10.200.0.4. When named is started directly with
`named -c /etc/bind/named.conf`, port 53 binds immediately and
`nslookup web0.local`/`nslookup web1.local` both resolve correctly from
host_1_1_1_1.

Root cause: the named DNS daemon crashed/exited on dns_server and was not
restarted, making port 53 unreachable and breaking all name resolution for the
campus.

Corrective fix: on dns_server, run `named -c /etc/bind/named.conf` (or add a
supervisor/watchdog entry to /startup so the daemon is reliably re-launched on
container start). No routing or OSPF changes are needed.
=== END ===
```